Yep. I worked it from the product backward through physiology, evidence, safety, claims, format, branding, manufacturing, and business model. I would not launch this as another “miracle lactation herb.” There is a better product hiding inside the idea.

MOMMA MILK™

Daily lactation nutrition for breastfeeding moms

Product thesis: a convenient single-serve powder stick/pouch built around the nutritional demands of breastfeeding, with hydration as the carrier and evidence-based maternal nutrition layered into it.

The positioning is:

Feed the mom who feeds the baby.

Not “this packet magically makes milk.” The physiological system makes milk; we’re supplying useful inputs while avoiding ingredients with questionable evidence or unnecessary risk.

1. The actual product

I’d launch Momma Milk Daily as a flavored powder stick pack mixed into roughly 16–24 oz of water.

V1 concept formulation, subject to formulation/safety review:

Component	Target concept	Purpose
Sodium	~250–400 mg	Hydration/electrolyte replacement
Potassium	~200–300 mg	Electrolyte support
Magnesium	~50–100 mg	Nutritional/electrolyte support
Choline	~150–250 mg	Helps supplement increased lactation requirement
Iodine	Carefully formulated dose	Lactation-associated nutritional need
Vitamin D	Moderate dose	Maternal nutrition
B12/B6/folate	Conservative doses	Maternal micronutrient support
Vitamin C	~60–100 mg	Nutritional support
Flavor + acid system	—	Makes drinking water easier/more enjoyable
Sugar	0–5 g	Depending on positioning

I would not put caffeine in the flagship product. That allows Momma Milk to be used during a 2 a.m. feeding just as easily as a noon pumping session.

CDC notes that breastfeeding women generally need roughly 330–400 additional kcal/day, and requirements for some micronutrients rise during lactation. 

Importantly, this isn’t intended to replace those calories. That creates our second product eventually: Momma Milk Fuel, a genuinely nutritious lactation snack.

2. What I would deliberately leave OUT

Here’s where we can differentiate from the supplement aisle.

No fenugreek in V1.

Fenugreek has become synonymous with lactation products, but the evidence isn’t particularly compelling. NCCIH describes the results as mixed and says better-quality research is needed. LactMed similarly reports conflicting evidence and notes limited safety data in nursing mothers and infants. It can also cause GI symptoms, allergic reactions and potentially affect blood glucose. 

That’s actually a marketing opportunity:

FENUGREEK FREE

Not because fenugreek is necessarily bad, but because we don’t need to build the entire product around a weakly established galactagogue.

I’d also avoid kitchen-sink proprietary herbal blends. They make safety, interactions, palatability, sourcing and substantiation harder.

3. The scientific principle

Milk production isn’t simply:

take ingredient → produce milk

A more realistic model is:

Milk\ Output =
f(Milk\ Removal,\ Hormonal\ Signaling,\ Maternal\ Physiology,\ Nutrition,\ Hydration,\ Time)

Milk removal/frequency and underlying breastfeeding mechanics matter enormously. LactMed explicitly cautions that galactagogues shouldn’t replace evaluation of modifiable factors affecting milk production. 

That gives Momma Milk a surprisingly good brand philosophy:

Don’t promise to hack lactation. Support the system doing the work.

That’s scientifically more defensible and creates consumer trust.

