7. Claims strategy matters enormously

I would not initially put:

INCREASES BREAST MILK

on the package.

That creates a much harder substantiation problem.

FDA permits dietary-supplement structure/function claims, but manufacturers must possess substantiation that the claim is truthful and non-misleading; qualifying claims require the statutory disclaimer and FDA notification no later than 30 days after first marketing. 

Much safer territory to have regulatory counsel/formulation experts evaluate would be language such as:

“Nutritional support for breastfeeding moms.”

“Supports hydration during lactation.”

“Helps meet increased nutritional needs during breastfeeding.”

Specific nutrient claims would then be substantiated against the actual formulation and applicable labeling rules.

FDA distinguishes structure/function claims from disease claims and other regulated health claims, so final package language needs a proper regulatory review rather than us freelancing the label. 

⸻

8. Where the milk-increase idea eventually comes back

Here’s the clever part.

Don’t abandon your original hypothesis.

Instrument it.

After V1 establishes customers, run a properly designed study:

baseline pumping output → standardized product use → repeated output measurements

Control as much as practicable for pumping schedule, postpartum stage, feeding pattern, hydration, etc.

Then analyze:

\Delta Milk = Milk_{intervention}-Milk_{baseline}

versus an appropriate control.

If a meaningful effect exists, earn the stronger claim rather than inventing it first.

That gives Momma Milk a research flywheel competitors built primarily around folklore may not have.

